\import{fdip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fdip}
\taxon{Definition}
\title{Ordered Berezin coefficients}
\date{2026-11-01}

\p{Let #{V} be an #{N}-dimensional vector space over
#{\mathbb k=\mathbb R} or #{\mathbb C}. The
\newvocab{Grassmann algebra} is the
[exterior algebra](ca-000E) #{\Lambda(V^*)}. Its dimension is
#{2^N}. A homogeneous element of degree #{r} is one in the
summand #{\Lambda^r(V^*)}. Elements of even degree commute with
every homogeneous element; two homogeneous elements of degrees
#{r,s} commute with sign #{(-1)^{rs}}.}

\p{Let #{\omega} be a nonzero element of the top line
#{\Lambda^N(V^*)}. The \newvocab{Berezin integral} #{I_\omega}
is the linear functional sending #{f} to the unique scalar for
which the degree-#{N} component of #{f} equals
#{I_\omega(f)\,\omega}. It vanishes on all lower degrees. For
#{N=0} the top line is #{\mathbb k} and #{I_\omega} is the
identity. This coefficient depends on the supplied volume. It is
not a Berezinian, and it is not a scalar Pfaffian. It is a
coefficient functional, not a positive measure on ordinary field
configurations. \ref{fdip-00MB} recovers the reversed word chart.}

\p{For a finite-dimensional complex space #{E}, the paired coefficient
on #{\Lambda(E^*\oplus E)} is the
[intrinsic paired coefficient](fdip-00IS). Its bars label
independent generators, not complex conjugates. The unpaired
volume #{I_\omega} and that paired coefficient are distinct.
\ref{fdip-00MC} recovers the interleaved word chart.}

\p{For a positive-degree nilpotent element #{q}, define
#{e^q=\sum_{r\ge0}q^r/r!}; only finitely many terms survive.
For a quadratic #{q} in #{N} generators, terms with #{2r>N}
vanish. No analytic convergence is involved.}

\transclude{fdip-00MB}
\transclude{fdip-00MC}
